  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Earthquake response in Mandalay and Sagaing Region (DRA)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11.0pt; line-height: 115%; font-family: &apos;Calibri&apos;,sans-serif; mso-ascii-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-fareast-font-family: Calibri; mso-fareast-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-hansi-theme-font: minor-latin; mso-bidi-font-family: &apos;Myanmar Text&apos;; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-bidi; mso-ansi-language: EN-US; mso-fareast-language: EN-US; mso-bidi-language: AR-SA;&quot;&gt;On March 28, 2025, Myanmar was struck by powerful earthquakes of magnitudes 7.7 and 6.4, marking the strongest seismic events in the country since 1946.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span lang=&quot;NL&quot; style=&quot;mso-bidi-font-family: Calibri; mso-bidi-theme-font: minor-latin;&quot;&gt;The impact of the earthquakes has been devastating across multiple regions. In Mandalay, Myanmar&amp;rsquo;s second-largest city, media reports indicate that numerous buildings have crumbled, while in Naypyidaw, the capital, roads have buckled, further complicating relief operations. In response to the crisis, authorities have declared a state of emergency across six regions, affecting more than 28 million people, including an estimated 6.7 million children.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;The earthquake struck a nation already in turmoil due to over four years of civil conflict triggered by the military coup in 2021. The situation demands urgent international assistance to address immediate humanitarian needs, including medical aid, shelter, and food supplies for millions affected by this catastrophe.&lt;/p&gt;</narrative>
  </description>
